@page "/permissions"
@inject ScryQuery Query
@inject IHttpClientFactory Clients

<PageTitle>Scry Sample — Cached policy</PageTitle>

<h1>Cached row policy</h1>
<p><a href="/">← Samples</a> · <a href="/scry">Explorer</a></p>
<p>
    Orders are scoped by a policy whose decision is too slow to run per row in SQL — it asks another
    system which regions this caller may see. So it runs in C# instead, once per row, and the server
    remembers the answers; the query carries only a membership test over the keys this caller is
    allowed. The counter below is what that saves: it goes up when a decision is actually made.
</p>

@if (error is not null)
{
    <p class="error" role="alert">@error</p>
}

<h2>Orders this caller can see</h2>
@if (orders is null)
{
    <p class="loading">Loading…</p>
}
else
{
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Region</th><th>Amount</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        @foreach (var order in orders)
        {
            <tr><td>@order.Region</td><td>@order.Amount</td></tr>
        }
        </tbody>
    </table>
}

<p>Expensive decisions made so far: <strong id="decisions">@decisions</strong></p>

<div class="pager">
    <button id="reload" @onclick="Reload">Run the query again</button>
    <button id="revise" disabled="@(orders is null or [])" @onclick="Revise">Revise an order</button>
</div>

<h3>Grants</h3>
<p>
    Revoking a region changes nothing about any order, so no version column could notice. The server
    tells the cache, and the next query decides every row again — without that call the change would
    never reach a query at all.
</p>
@if (grants is not null)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var region in grants.Regions)
        {
            <li>
                <label>
                    <input type="checkbox"
                           id="@($"grant-{region}")"
                           checked="@grants.Granted.Contains(region)"
                           @onchange="_ => Set(region, (bool)_.Value!)"/>
                    @region
                </label>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
}

<h3>What to try</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Run the query again.</strong> The rows come back and the counter does not move: every row was already decided.</li>
    <li><strong>Revise an order.</strong> Its revision moves past the watermark this caller was decided up to, so the next query decides that one row — the counter goes up by exactly one. A row inserted by any writer is correct on its first read the same way.</li>
    <li><strong>Revoke a region.</strong> The rows disappear and the counter jumps by the whole set: the caller's answers were thrown away and made again.</li>
</ul>
